  "answer": "Yes—as an examination step. However, the supplied passages do not establish the procedure or timing for revocation.\n\n**Manual practice:** The Manual expressly allows the examiner to request additional information during examination to clearly define the scope of a movement mark, keeping section 65 in mind. It does not make that request conditional on starting revocation proceedings. [TMM/Part10/3/3/6/3] The relevant material includes clear representations, a description showing all features and a copy of the actual mark, such as a video clip. [TMM/Part21/9#1]\n\n**Outside information:** If the examiner believes that information establishes a ground for rejection, Manual practice requires the ground to be specific and well documented if possible. It cautions against unsupported assertions and says that references used should be supplied to the applicant. Doubts should therefore not simply be treated as an established ground without that assessment. [TMM/Part22/7#1]\n\n**Act—not merely practice:** Clarification must respect the statutory amendment limits. After publication under section 30, section 65(2) permits amendment of the representation only if it does not substantially affect the mark’s identity. Section 65(7) prohibits amendments to other particulars that would extend the rights obtainable on registration. [TMA1995/s65]\n\n**Limit:** The passages support requesting clarification during examination, but contain no revocation provisions. I cannot establish from them whether a particular pre-revocation process permits or requires such a request."
